Hölder's inequality states that for integrable functions \( u \) and \( v \) and for \( \frac{1}{p} + \frac{1}{q} = 1 \),  
\[ \int_0^\infty |u(x)v(x)| \, dx \leq \left( \int_0^\infty |u(x)|^p \, dx \right)^{1/p} \left( \int_0^\infty |v(x)|^q \, dx \right)^{1/q}. \]  
Using \( p = 3 \) and \( q = 3/2 \) gives us:  
\[ \frac{1}{3} + \frac{2}{3} = 1. \]  
Let \( u(x) = f(x) \) and \( v(x) = x^{2/3} \), so  
\[ \int_0^\infty |f(x)x^{2/3}| \, dx \leq \left( \int_0^\infty f(x)^3 \, dx \right)^{1/3} \left( \int_0^\infty x^2 \, dx \right)^{1/3}. \]

**Answer:**  
\( \int_0^\infty f(x)x^{2/3} \, dx \leq \left( \int_0^\infty f(x)^3 \, dx \right)^{1/3} \left( \int_0^\infty x^2 \, dx \right)^{1/3}. \)
